# Pins for the integration-e2e workflow: which chart, testdata and server build the
# player is tested against. Sourced as shell variables by workflow.yml.
#
# Keep these hard so player builds are replayable:
# - CHART_REF: a chart release tag (vX.Y.Z) or commit sha of ister-app/chart.
# - TESTDATA_REF: a commit sha of ister-app/testdata (fixtures have no releases).
# - SERVER_IMAGE_TAG: normally EMPTY — then the server (and migrations) version comes
#   from the chart itself (its pinned appVersion/server.image.tag), which is the version
#   the chart was released and tested with. Only set it to try the player against a
#   different server build: a release version ("1.1.0") or the next snapshot
#   ("1.2.0-snapshot", published by the server repo's docker-publish workflow on every
#   push to main — note that a -SNAPSHOT tag is mutable and moves with main).
#
# Includes the widened internal-db probe timeouts — without them a busy CI node
# gets postgres killed by its liveness probe mid-tour (doc_tour flaked on this).
# b1ff654 publishes the e2e API port (kind extraPortMappings + ci/nodeports.yaml)
# instead of tunnelling it through `kubectl port-forward`, whose stream starts
# timing out requests under the HLS load of this suite — the subtitle-seek test
# hung three minutes on a GraphQL call that never came back.
# bac9a05 renames the e2e's scan call to scanLibraries (server 3.0.0 dropped
# scanLibrary) and pulls the chart's own server image up to 3.0.0 — b1ff654 fails
# 10-scan.sh outright against the server pinned below.
CHART_REF=bac9a055c7579c0639d49513f86a392da9c6f368
# ddc5196 generates a synthetic Dragonfly s01e08 with embedded SRT subtitles —
# the subtitle-seek e2e's fixture. Before it, that episode only existed locally
# as a gitignored DVD rip: in CI the episode had no media file, the episode page
# silently skipped auto-start and the test timed out waiting for playback.
TESTDATA_REF=ddc5196a063bb7aab24aca647cb3e63ec8338e8f
# The 2.8.1-snapshot line brings the devices schema (registerDevice/myDevices/
# pingDevice/sendDeviceCommand, the deviceCommands subscription and the
# updatePlayQueue deviceId argument + PlaybackSession device fields), which the
# player now sends/selects (an older server fails the whole query on the unknown
# field/argument). The same line adds the follower list (sessionFollowers /
# removeFollower), PlaybackCommand.targetDeviceId and the session repeat mode
# (updatePlayQueue's repeatMode argument, PlaybackSession.repeatMode and the
# SET_REPEAT command), all of which the player sends/selects — so remote control
# and the playback heartbeat need this server too.
# The 2.9.1-snapshot line adds Book.progress (whole-book reading/listening position),
# which the continue-watching row and the book page now select — an older server fails
# the whole recentlyWatched query on the unknown field.
# If the predicted patch version never gets released (feat commits bump the server's
# minor instead), the release gate falls back to the closest newer release in the same
# major and pins that; only a major bump still needs a manual re-point.
# The 2.10.2-snapshot line adds the STOP playback command (end the whole session from
# a stop button / mini-player swipe / remote control), which the player now publishes
# on stopPlayback and executes from the command bus. (It was first pinned as
# 2.10.1-snapshot, but the server released v2.10.1 before the STOP commit landed, so
# that snapshot tag froze on a pre-STOP image and the suite failed on the unknown
# enum value.)
# 2.13.0 brings DeviceCommand.targetDeviceId (HANDOFF_QUEUE pull handoff — the
# deviceCommands subscription selects it, and an older server fails the whole
# subscription on the unknown field, which killed every e2e run on 2.11.0) and
# Episode.mediaFileParts/MediaFilePart (multi-episode files), both of which the
# player now selects.
# The 2.13.1-snapshot line adds MediaFileStream.cropX/cropY/cropWidth/cropHeight
# (baked-in black-bar crop detection), which the player now selects — an older
# server fails the whole media-file query on the unknown fields.
# The 2.14.1-snapshot line adds MediaFile.segments (season-wide intro/outro
# detection) and UserSettings.autoSkipIntro, both of which the player now
# selects — an older server fails the media-file and user-settings queries on
# the unknown fields. The segment_skip e2e also asserts the detected bounds of
# the Cicada fixture show, which this testdata pin introduces. (2.13.1-snapshot
# froze pre-segments when v2.14.0 released before the detection commits landed.)
# The 2.14.1-snapshot tag (still current — the server hasn't released since) now
# also carries ProcessingItem.subject/step, the TRANSCODE_ACTIVITY event type with
# ServerActivityEvent.transcodes/TranscodePass and ServerActivitySnapshot.transcodes —
# all of which the activity screen now selects, so an older server image fails the
# serverActivity subscription and the snapshot query on the unknown fields. The
# eventual release will be 2.15.0 (feat commits); the release gate's same-major
# fallback resolves 2.14.1 to that.
# albums(appearsOnArtistId:) (the person page's "Appears on" grid) shipped in 2.17.0. The
# 2.17.1-snapshot line adds Person.recentlyAddedTracks + Track.dateAdded (the "Recently
# added" tab) and RankKind.RECENTLY_ADDED (that tab's ARTIST play queue) — an older server
# fails the query on the unknown field and rejects the enum value. (First pinned as
# 2.16.2-snapshot, but v2.17.0 released before that commit landed, so that tag froze
# without it.) Released as 2.18.0 (feat commits); the same-major fallback resolves this
# snapshot to that.
# The 2.19.1-snapshot line adds the per-item playback history (the playbackHistory
# query with WatchStatus.createdAt/updatedAt, and the markPlayed/deleteWatchStatus
# mutations), which the history sheet on every detail page now sends — an older
# server rejects those operations. Landed just after v2.19.0 released, so the
# eventual release is 2.20.0 (feat commit); the same-major fallback resolves this
# snapshot to that.
# The 2.20.1-snapshot line adds UserSettings.hideSubtitlesMatchingAudio: leave subtitles
# off when the language that would be picked is the one already being spoken. The player
# selects it in fragmentUserSettings and sends it on every save, so an older server fails
# the whole userSettings query on the unknown field and rejects the mutation's unknown
# input field — the language settings page stops working entirely against one. v2.20.0
# released while this was being written, freezing 2.19.1-snapshot before the commit
# landed, so this had to move to the new snapshot line rather than ride the old tag. The
# eventual release is 2.21.0 (feat commits); the release gate's same-major fallback
# resolves this snapshot to that.
# That 2.21.0 never came: the server released v3.0.0 instead — feat(api)!: redesign the
# maintenance flows around scanLibraries/refreshMetadata/rebuildSearchIndex, the API the
# player's own maintenance actions now call. The release gate only falls back within the
# same major (a major bump is a breaking change and must be re-pointed by hand), so the
# 2.20.1-snapshot pin failed the nightly release outright. v3.0.0 carries everything the
# snapshot lines above were pinned for — hideSubtitlesMatchingAudio, the extended TMDB
# metadata and the maintenance redesign — so this points at the release itself rather
# than the next snapshot line.
# The 3.0.1-snapshot line carries two server features the player now sends. Show.related
# (the related-shows row on the show page) landed first, in its own query, so against
# 3.0.0 that row fails while the rest of the show page keeps working. Then came
# trackPlaybackHistory(scope: ALBUM|ARTIST) with WatchStatus.track — the playback history
# of a whole album or artist, which the album and artist pages now open from their menus;
# an older server rejects the operation outright. Both are feat commits on top of
# v3.0.0, so the eventual release is 3.1.0 and the release gate's same-major fallback
# resolves this snapshot to it.
SERVER_IMAGE_TAG=3.0.1-snapshot
